"""Adult course, units 15 and 16: reading the Bible with the Church, and the moral life."""
from spec import L, U

B = [
U('Reading the Bible with the Church', 'Revelation', 'How do I read Scripture so that it feeds me instead of confusing me?', [
 L('Reading the Old Testament, including the hard parts', 'The Old Testament is the first part of one story that Christ completes. Catholics read it in light of Christ, attend to what the human author meant, and accept that some passages show a people still learning who God is.', '121,122,123,124,128,129,130,115,116,117,118,119,120,2585', 'Luke 24:44-47', 'old-testament typology senses-of-scripture covenant salvation-history', 'How should I read the violent or strange parts of the Old Testament?', 'Pick one hard passage. Write what it literally says, what it meant for its first readers, and how the New Testament lights it.', '',
   teach=['The Church holds that the Old Testament is true Word of God, and that the New Testament lies hidden in the Old while the Old is made plain in the New (CCC 129). Jesus himself read the Law, the Prophets and the Psalms as speaking of him (Luke 24:44). Marcion’s attempt in the second century to throw out the Old Testament was rejected as heresy.', 'The Church reads Scripture in four senses (CCC 115–119): the literal sense, which the human author intended, and three spiritual senses: allegorical (how events point to Christ), moral (how they teach us to act) and anagogical (how they point to heaven). The literal sense comes first, and the others rest on it. This means asking about genre, history and context before drawing lessons.', 'For the hard passages, the Church offers three helps: God teaches gradually (CCC 53, 122), so the laws and wars of early Israel are not the last word; the Bible tells what happened without approving everything it reports; and the whole must be read in the light of Christ’s death and resurrection (CCC 112–114). Seek a good study Bible and the Church’s tradition, and bring honest questions.'],
   talk=['Which Old Testament passage has troubled or puzzled you most?', 'What difference does it make to read it in the light of Christ?', 'Why does the Church ask for the literal sense first?'],
   misc=['“The God of the Old Testament is a different God.”', '“Whatever the Bible reports, it approves.”'],
   quiz=['What does the Church teach about the Old Testament?|It is obsolete|It is true Word of God, fulfilled in Christ|It is only history|It is optional|1|CCC 121–123.', 'Which sense of Scripture comes first?|Allegorical|Literal|Moral|Anagogical|1|CCC 116.', 'What is the principle of gradual revelation?|God taught his people step by step|God changed his mind|Scripture contradicts itself|Only the New Testament matters|0|CCC 53.'],
   life='Read one chapter from Exodus this week with a study Bible, and write down what you learn about who God is.', saint='Ambrose of Milan: the bishop whose preaching on the Old Testament, reading it as pointing to Christ, opened Scripture to Augustine.',
   deeper=['apol:suffering'], mem='“These are my words which I spoke to you while I was still with you, that everything written about me in the law of Moses and the prophets and the psalms must be fulfilled.” (Luke 24:44)', hook='Ask: “What do you do with a passage you do not like?” Write answers; invite honesty.', assess='Ask each person to name the four senses of Scripture and give one example of gradual revelation.',
   tryit=['Choose a hard Old Testament passage and write its literal meaning.', 'Look up its context in a study Bible.', 'Find one New Testament verse that sheds light on it.'],
   saintStory='Ambrose was born around 339 in Trier, to a Roman official’s family, and became a lawyer and then governor of the province of Liguria and Emilia, based in Milan. In 374 the bishop of Milan died, and the Arians and Catholics were arguing over his successor. Ambrose came to calm the crowd, and a child’s voice, it is said, shouted “Ambrose for bishop.” He was only a catechumen. He was baptized, and consecrated bishop within a week.\nHe studied Scripture deeply with the help of the priest Simplician and the Greek Fathers, especially Origen. His sermons on Genesis, the Psalms and the Gospel of Luke read the Old Testament as pointing to Christ. He was a fearless pastor: he refused to give a church to the Arian empress Justina, and stood against Emperor Theodosius, who had ordered a massacre at Thessalonica in 390, and made him do public penance before he could receive Communion.\nAugustine, then a teacher of rhetoric in Milan, went to hear Ambrose for his style and was struck by his substance: the stories he had mocked in the Old Testament were made plain when read spiritually. Ambrose baptized him at Easter 387. Ambrose died on April 4, 397, and is one of the four original Doctors of the Western Church.',
   script=dict(open='Write on the board three things that people find “hard” in the Old Testament, as you have heard them. Invite additions from the group. Say, “None of these questions is off limits tonight. We are learning how the Church reads, not hiding from the page.”',
     teach=['Say: “The Church treats the Old Testament as the Word of God. The Catechism says, ‘The Old Testament is an indispensable part of Sacred Scripture. Its books are divinely inspired and retain a permanent value’ (CCC 121). The New Testament lies hidden in the Old, and the Old is unveiled in the New (CCC 129). Jesus on the Emmaus road interprets for his disciples ‘in all the Scriptures the things concerning himself’ (Luke 24:27). In the second century, Marcion claimed the God of the Old Testament was a different, lesser God and wanted to throw it out; the Church condemned this.”', 'Say: “How do we read? Look at the four senses (CCC 115–119). The literal sense is what the human author meant, and we find it by attention to genre, culture and history. The allegorical sense reads events as pointing to Christ: the Exodus, the Passover lamb, Jonah’s three days. The moral sense teaches us how to act. The anagogical sense points us toward heaven: Jerusalem as the heavenly city. An old rhyme sums it up: the letter teaches what happened, allegory what you believe, the moral what you do, anagogy where you are going. Always start with the letter.”', 'Say: “The hard passages. Three principles. First, gradual revelation: God does not overwhelm his people but leads them as a teacher leads a child (CCC 53, 64, 122). Second, the Bible reports as well as endorses; the sins of David and the violence of some stories are told truthfully and are not approved. Third, read the whole canon in light of Christ (CCC 112–114): the unity of Scripture. Practical: use a good Catholic study Bible, such as the Ignatius or Navarre editions, and a trusted guide; bring the question to a priest or teacher.”'],
     ask=['What are the four senses of Scripture? (Literal, allegorical, moral, anagogical.)', 'Which one comes first? (The literal.)', 'Why does the Church reject Marcion’s view? (The same God inspired both testaments.)'],
     practice=['Read Luke 24:13–35 and mark what Jesus does with the Old Testament.', 'In pairs, take one hard passage and apply the three principles.', 'Each writes a brief answer to “Isn’t the God of the Old Testament angry?” and shares it.'],
     close='Pray Psalm 119:105 together: “Your word is a lamp to my feet and a light to my path.” Ask the group to bring one hard passage next week.')),
 L('Reading the Gospels', 'The four Gospels are the heart of Scripture because they give us the life, words and death of Christ. The Church holds them to be historical and also to be written by believers for believers, in three stages from Jesus to the page.', '124,125,126,127,512,513,514,515,516,517,518,519', 'Luke 1:1-4', 'gospel apostle inspired inerrancy tradition', 'Can I trust what the Gospels say about Jesus, and how should I read them?', 'Read the same story, such as the feeding of the five thousand, in all four Gospels. List the details they share and the details only one has.', '',
   teach=['The Church teaches that the Gospels faithfully hand on what Jesus did and taught for our salvation (Dei Verbum 19; CCC 126). They were formed in three stages (CCC 126): the life and teaching of Jesus; the oral tradition of the apostles after Easter, adapted to their listeners; and the written Gospels, in which the authors selected, arranged and explained, always keeping the truth about Jesus.', 'Why four? Matthew, Mark, Luke and John each shape the story for a purpose and an audience. Matthew shows Jesus as the new Moses fulfilling Israel’s Scriptures; Mark as the suffering Son of God; Luke as the Savior of the poor and outcast; John as the eternal Word made flesh. Matthew, Mark and Luke are called the Synoptics because they can be set side by side. Differences are not contradictions. They are what we expect from different witnesses.', 'Practical ways to read: read a whole Gospel in one sitting; read slowly with a Catholic study Bible; ask what the passage says, what it meant, what it means for Christ, and what it asks of me; pray with a scene by imagining yourself in it (Ignatian contemplation). Remember the Gospels are proclaimed at Mass, with the Church, for the Church (CCC 127).'],
   talk=['Which Gospel do you know best, and which least?', 'How does it help to know that the Gospels were written by believers who also wanted to be accurate?', 'Which scene would you like to picture yourself in?'],
   misc=['“The Gospels contradict each other.”', '“They were written centuries later by people who never knew Jesus.”'],
   quiz=['Into how many stages does the Church teach the Gospels were formed?|One|Two|Three|Four|2|CCC 126.', 'Which three Gospels are called the Synoptics?|Matthew, Mark, Luke|Luke, John, Acts|Mark, John, Luke|Matthew, John, Acts|0|They can be set side by side.', 'Why four Gospels?|Each presents Jesus with its own emphasis for its audience|The Church could not decide|They are copies|Only one is true|0|CCC 127.'],
   life='Read the Gospel of Mark in one sitting this week, which takes about an hour and a half, and note anything that surprises you.', saint='Luke the Evangelist: the physician and companion of Paul who researched his Gospel carefully from eyewitnesses and wrote the Acts of the Apostles.',
   deeper=['apol:sola-scriptura'], mem='“It seemed good to me also, having followed all things closely for some time past, to write an orderly account for you.” (Luke 1:3)', hook='Ask: “If you had to explain in one sentence who Jesus is, using only one Gospel, which would you choose and why?”', assess='Ask each person to list the three stages of formation of the Gospels and give a key emphasis for each Gospel.',
   tryit=['Read the feeding of the five thousand in all four Gospels.', 'Make a list of the shared and distinct details.', 'Write one line on what each author seems to emphasize.'],
   saintStory='Luke was probably born in Antioch in Syria and was a Gentile, the only non-Jewish author in the New Testament. Paul calls him “the beloved physician” (Colossians 4:14). Tradition says he was a Greek-speaking doctor, and his Gospel and Acts show an educated author with a fine Greek style and an interest in medical detail. He joined Paul on the second missionary journey, and the “we” passages in Acts suggest he was present at Philippi and later on the voyage to Rome.\nWhen Paul was imprisoned in Rome, Luke stayed with him. “Only Luke is with me,” Paul writes near the end (2 Timothy 4:11). In his preface he says he has investigated everything carefully from the beginning and is writing an orderly account. Only Luke has the parables of the Good Samaritan and the Prodigal Son, the stories of Zacchaeus, the repentant thief, and the Annunciation and Visitation, which would have come from Mary or those close to her. He pays attention to women, the poor, and sinners.\nLater tradition says he died at about eighty-four in Boeotia in Greece, unmarried, and that he painted icons of Mary, though that is a legend from many centuries after. His feast is October 18, and he is patron of physicians, artists and surgeons.',
   script=dict(open='Pose a quick poll: raise a hand if you have ever read a whole Gospel from start to finish. Then ask what stopped you, or what kept you going. Say, “Tonight we get a map for the four Gospels and a method for reading them.”',
     teach=['Say: “The Gospels are the heart of all the Scriptures because they are our principal source for the life and teaching of the Incarnate Word, our Savior (CCC 125). Dei Verbum 19 says the Church holds that the four Gospels ‘whose historicity she unhesitatingly affirms, faithfully hand on what Jesus the Son of God, while he lived among men, really did and taught for their eternal salvation.’ This is not a claim that they are modern biographies. They are testimony, written to bring us to faith (John 20:31).”', 'Say: “The three stages (CCC 126). First, the life and teaching of Jesus. Second, the oral tradition: the apostles preached what Jesus said and did, adjusting to their hearers and with the greater understanding they had after Easter and Pentecost. Third, the written Gospels, in which the evangelists selected from the tradition, summarized, explained, and arranged, always telling the honest truth about Jesus. Luke tells us this directly in his prologue. This explains why accounts differ in detail and agree in substance. Mark is probably earliest, around AD 65–70; Matthew and Luke seem to use it and a common source; John is distinct.”', 'Say: “How to read. Slowly, in whole scenes. Ask four questions: What does the text say? What was it saying to its first hearers? What does it say about Christ? What does it ask of me? A good Catholic study Bible gives background. Pray with a scene by Ignatian contemplation: picture the setting, the people, and enter as an observer. Read the Gospel of the coming Sunday during the week. And never forget the Gospels belong in the Church’s worship: we stand for the Gospel at Mass because Christ speaks in it (CCC 127).”'],
     ask=['What are the three stages of the formation of the Gospels? (Jesus’ life, oral tradition, written Gospels.)', 'What does each Gospel emphasize? (Matthew: fulfilment; Mark: suffering Son of God; Luke: mercy to the poor; John: the Word made flesh.)', 'What are four questions for reading a passage? (What does it say, what did it mean, what does it say of Christ, what does it ask of me?)'],
     practice=['Read Luke 1:1–4 aloud and list what Luke says he did.', 'In groups of three, each take a Gospel’s version of the feeding of the five thousand and report on its distinct details.', 'Choose a Gospel scene and write five sentences from the point of view of an onlooker.'],
     close='Pray the Our Father together, then read a short Gospel passage slowly, with a minute of silence after.')),
 L('Praying the Psalms', 'The 150 Psalms were the prayer book of Israel and of Jesus, and are the backbone of the Church’s daily prayer. They teach us to bring everything to God: joy, anger, doubt, repentance and praise.', '2585,2586,2587,2588,2589,2596,2597,1174,1175,1176,1177,1178,2700,2701,2702', 'Psalm 130', 'prayer liturgy-of-hours praise petition lectio-divina', 'How do I pray the Psalms, and what do I do with the ones that anger or confuse me?', 'Pray Psalm 130 slowly each morning for a week. Write one line about what you notice each day.', '',
   teach=['The Psalter is the book in which the Word of God becomes the prayer of man (CCC 2587). Jesus prayed the Psalms, from Psalm 22 on the Cross to the hymn at the Last Supper (Matthew 26:30). The Church prays them in the Mass, in the Liturgy of the Hours, and in private devotion. They are poems, with parallelism, images and sudden turns of mood.', 'The Psalms are of different kinds (CCC 2589): hymns of praise; laments, individual and communal; thanksgiving; royal and messianic psalms, which the Church reads as pointing to Christ; wisdom psalms; pilgrim songs (Psalms 120–134); and penitential psalms (6, 32, 38, 51, 102, 130, 143). The Psalms teach honesty with God: even Psalm 88 ends in darkness.', 'The hard Psalms. Some call for vengeance (Psalm 137:9, 109). The Church does not edit the Bible, but reads them in Christ: the enemy is also sin and death; we bring our anger to God rather than act on it; and in the Liturgy of the Hours the verses that are hardest are omitted or marked, for pastoral reasons. To pray the Psalms, read slowly, aloud, pausing at the verse that catches you, and let the words give you the prayer you cannot find.'],
   talk=['Which Psalm do you know by heart or return to in trouble?', 'What does it mean to pray an angry or sorrowful Psalm?', 'How might the Psalms give shape to your day?'],
   misc=['“Psalms are only for monks and clergy.”', '“You must feel something to pray.”'],
   quiz=['How many Psalms are there?|50|100|150|200|2|The Psalter.', 'Which of these is a penitential Psalm?|Psalm 23|Psalm 51|Psalm 150|Psalm 1|1|Psalm 51 (Miserere).', 'What is the Liturgy of the Hours?|A daily cycle of prayer built on the Psalms|A parish office|A type of hymn|A holiday|0|CCC 1174–1178.'],
   life='Choose a Psalm for morning (such as Psalm 63) and one for evening (such as Psalm 141). Pray them each day this week.', saint='Athanasius of Alexandria: in a letter to Marcellinus he described the Psalms as a mirror in which every person finds their own movements of soul.',
   deeper=[], mem='“Out of the depths I cry to you, O Lord! Lord, hear my voice!” (Psalm 130:1–2)', hook='Read Psalm 130 slowly aloud in silence-bounded stages. Ask: “Which line caught you?”', assess='Ask each person to name three kinds of Psalm and explain how to pray a difficult Psalm.',
   tryit=['Pray Psalm 130 slowly and note the line that stays with you.', 'Mark one Psalm of praise and one of lament in your Bible.', 'Set up a time and place to pray one Psalm each morning.'],
   saintStory='Athanasius was born around 296 in Alexandria, Egypt, and as a young deacon accompanied his bishop Alexander to the Council of Nicaea in 325, where the Church defined that the Son is of one substance with the Father. At thirty-some he became bishop of Alexandria in 328, and spent the next forty-five years defending Nicaea against the Arians, who had the emperors’ support. He was exiled five times, spending about seventeen years away from his see, including time hidden among the desert monks in Egypt.\nHis writings include On the Incarnation, the Life of Antony, and Festal Letters. In his Letter to Marcellinus on the Psalms, he writes that most of Scripture speaks to us, but the Psalter also speaks for us: whoever recites the Psalms finds in them the movements of his own soul, and can use their words as if they were his own. He recommends praying the Psalms for every situation: thanksgiving, repentance, trouble, victory.\nHe died on May 2, 373, in Alexandria, in his bed, having outlasted the emperors who exiled him. He is a Doctor of the Church and is called “Athanasius contra mundum,” Athanasius against the world.',
   script=dict(open='Read Psalm 130 aloud, slowly, with a pause between the verses. Then say nothing for half a minute. Ask, “What did you notice?” Say, “Tonight we learn how to use these words as our own.”',
     teach=['Say: “The Psalter is the prayer book of the Bible. CCC 2587: ‘The Psalter is the book in which the Word of God becomes man’s prayer.’ Jesus prayed them: the Last Supper ended with a hymn (the Hallel, Psalms 113–118), and on the Cross he prayed Psalm 22. The early Church prayed the Psalms daily, and monastic communities prayed all 150 each week. Today the Liturgy of the Hours (CCC 1174–1178) spreads them over four weeks. Any layperson can pray Morning and Evening Prayer; apps, books and the Divine Office website make it easy.”', 'Say: “Kinds of psalms (CCC 2589). Praise: Psalms 8, 19, 29, 33, 100, 145–150. Lament: Psalms 13, 22, 42, 88. Thanksgiving: Psalms 30, 34, 116. Royal and messianic: Psalms 2, 72, 110. Wisdom: Psalms 1, 37, 73. Pilgrim songs: Psalms 120–134. Penitential: Psalms 6, 32, 38, 51, 102, 130, 143. Notice they bring everything, including anger and abandonment, to God. Psalm 88 has no hope at the end. This is not a failure of faith; it is faith telling the truth to God.”', 'Say: “The hard Psalms. Psalm 137 ends with a curse on Babylon’s children; Psalms 58 and 109 ask God to punish enemies. The Church’s answer: the Bible shows human emotion honestly; we give our anger to God rather than act on it; and the Church, praying in Christ, reads the enemies as the powers of sin and death. The Liturgy of the Hours leaves out a few of the most severe verses. A method for praying a Psalm: read slowly; pause where a line catches you; repeat it; speak to God in your own words; end with the Glory Be, which turns every Psalm toward the Trinity.”'],
     ask=['What did Jesus do with the Psalms? (He prayed them, from the Last Supper to the Cross.)', 'Name three kinds of Psalm. (Praise, lament, thanksgiving, royal, wisdom, pilgrim, penitential.)', 'How do we pray a difficult Psalm? (Honestly, in Christ, giving our anger to God.)'],
     practice=['Pray Psalm 23 together, a verse at a time, pausing after each.', 'In pairs, choose a Psalm that fits something happening this week and pray it for each other.', 'Plan a daily time and place for one Psalm and share the plan.'],
     close='Pray Psalm 130 together once more, then the Glory Be. Invite each person to start the morning and evening habit this week.')),
 ]),
U('Living the moral life', 'Morality', 'How do I form my conscience, build good habits and live the commandments with joy?', [
 L('Conscience and how to form it', 'Conscience is the judgment of reason that tells us what to do or avoid. It must be formed, because it can be mistaken, and we are bound to follow it after honest effort to learn the truth.', '1776,1777,1778,1779,1780,1781,1782,1783,1784,1785,1786,1787,1790,1791,1792,1793,1794,1795,1796', 'Romans 2:14-16', 'conscience moral-law natural-law free-will virtue', 'What does it mean to follow my conscience, and how do I know that it is right?', 'Write a brief examination of conscience for one area of life, such as work, family or money, using the Commandments and Beatitudes as a guide.', '',
   teach=['Conscience is a judgment of reason by which a person recognizes the moral quality of a concrete act (CCC 1778). It is not a feeling, nor a vote, nor the voice of society. In the depths of conscience, the Council says, a person finds a law which he did not give himself but which he must obey (Gaudium et Spes 16). The Church teaches that we must always obey a certain conscience (CCC 1790).', 'Because conscience can err, we have a duty to form it (CCC 1783–1785): by studying Scripture and the teaching of the Church, by examining ourselves, by prayer, by the advice of wise people, and by the practice of virtue. Ignorance may excuse when it is invincible, that is, not our own fault; but not when we have neglected to learn (CCC 1791–1793). A conscience can be lax, scrupulous, or hardened.', 'The Church distinguishes right conscience, which judges according to reason and the divine law, from erroneous conscience. Newman said conscience is the first of all the vicars of Christ, but that it is not a right to do as one likes (Letter to the Duke of Norfolk). For Catholics, forming conscience includes taking the Church’s teaching seriously as a teacher given by Christ, not as one opinion among many. An examination of conscience each night is the best habit.'],
   talk=['Where have you been tempted to say “my conscience says” when you meant “I want”?', 'What helps you hear your conscience clearly?', 'Who are the people who help you form it?'],
   misc=['“Conscience means doing whatever I feel is right.”', '“Following conscience means I cannot be wrong.”'],
   quiz=['What is conscience?|A feeling|A judgment of reason about the goodness of an act|A rule from the state|An opinion|1|CCC 1778.', 'Are we bound to form our conscience?|No|Yes, it is a lifelong duty|Only priests|Only children|1|CCC 1783.', 'What can excuse a wrong act done through ignorance?|Any ignorance|Only ignorance that is not our own fault|Nothing|Only youth|1|CCC 1793.'],
   life='Take five minutes each night this week for an examination of conscience: thank God, review the day, ask pardon, resolve.', saint='Franz Jägerstätter: an Austrian farmer who refused to swear loyalty to Hitler and was executed in 1943, after discerning, against the advice of his priest and bishop, that he could not serve in the army.',
   deeper=[], mem='“What the law requires is written on their hearts, while their conscience also bears witness.” (Romans 2:15)', hook='Ask: “When has your conscience bothered you? What was it asking you to do?” Allow quiet before answers.', assess='Ask each person to explain how conscience is formed and name two things that can distort it.',
   tryit=['Write a short examination of conscience for one area of life.', 'Do it for three nights.', 'Name one person you will ask for advice on a tough decision.'],
   saintStory='Franz Jägerstätter was born in 1907 in St. Radegund, in Upper Austria, the illegitimate son of a farm maid; he was raised by his grandmother and later adopted by his stepfather. He worked as a farmhand and a miner, and had a rough youth, with a fondness for motorcycles and brawls. In 1936 he married Franziska Schwaninger, a devout woman, and they had three daughters. He became more serious about his faith, took the sexton’s job at the village church, and joined the Third Order of St Francis.\nIn 1938, when Austria was annexed by Germany, he was the only person in his village to vote “no” in the plebiscite. From 1940 he refused to serve in the army, and in 1943 he was called up and refused to take the oath to Hitler. He believed he could not fight in an unjust war. His pastor and his bishop advised him to serve, saying a private person cannot judge the justice of a war; his wife supported him, though she did not want to lose him. He was imprisoned in Berlin and condemned to death.\nHe wrote to his family from prison, in peace, forgiving everyone. He was beheaded on August 9, 1943, aged thirty-six. After the war he was almost forgotten, but Vatican II’s recognition of conscientious objection, and the work of the American sociologist Gordon Zahn, brought attention to him. He was beatified in 2007 as a martyr.',
   script=dict(open='Ask everyone to think of a moment when they knew something was wrong before they could say why. Invite one or two to share. Say, “That inner sense is the subject tonight, and also the question of how far we can trust it.”',
     teach=['Say: “What is conscience? The Catechism (CCC 1776–1778) calls it the voice of God echoing in the heart, a judgment of practical reason that tells us at the right moment to do the good and avoid evil. It applies the moral law to a particular case. It has three moments: the knowledge of the principle, the application to this act, and the judgment on acts we have done. Conscience is not an inner oracle to be trusted blindly nor an invention of the person; it responds to a truth we did not make.”', 'Say: “We must obey a certain conscience (CCC 1790), but we must also form it (CCC 1783–1785). Education of conscience is a lifelong task: by the Word of God, by the teaching of the Church, by prayer and examination, by the guidance of wise people, and by the virtues. The Catechism admits that conscience may err through ignorance (CCC 1791–1794). If the ignorance is not our fault, the act is not imputable; but where we could have known better and did not take the trouble, we are responsible. Conscience may also be hardened by habitual sin (CCC 1791).”', 'Say: “Practical formation. One: the daily examen, the five-step prayer that Ignatius recommended: gratitude, light, review, sorrow, resolution. Two: bring hard decisions to someone wiser. Three: read the Church’s teaching on the matter, and its reasons, before deciding. Four: avoid both laxity (I can justify anything) and scrupulosity (I fear everything is sin); a spiritual director helps with both. Five: remember the Church’s principle that we may never do evil that good may come of it (CCC 1789). Franz Jägerstätter is an example of a formed conscience at great cost.”'],
     ask=['What is conscience? (A judgment of reason about the moral quality of an act.)', 'How do we form it? (Scripture, Church teaching, prayer, examination, advice, virtue.)', 'When is a person not responsible for a wrong act done by mistake? (When the ignorance is not their own fault.)'],
     practice=['Walk through the five steps of the examen together, in silence, as a model.', 'In pairs, describe a decision you face and what resources could help you form your conscience.', 'Draft your own short nightly examination of conscience and share one line.'],
     close='Pray together: “Lord, make me an instrument of your peace,” or a short act of contrition. Invite each person to do the examen for the rest of the week.')),
 L('Virtue: building the habits of a good life', 'Virtues are habits that make it easier to do good. The Church speaks of four human or cardinal virtues and three theological virtues, which God gives, and teaches that holiness is formed by practice and grace together.', '1803,1804,1805,1806,1807,1808,1809,1810,1811,1812,1813,1814,1815,1816,1817,1818,1819,1820,1821,1822,1823,1824,1825,1826,1827,1828,1829', 'Galatians 5:22-23', 'virtue cardinal-virtues theological-virtues prudence justice fortitude temperance', 'How do I actually become more patient, honest and generous, rather than just wishing I were?', 'Pick one virtue. Write three small concrete acts that would build it and do one each day this week.', '',
   teach=['A virtue is a habitual and firm disposition to do the good (CCC 1803). The four cardinal virtues are prudence, which finds the right means to a good end; justice, which gives to God and neighbor what is due; fortitude, which holds firm in difficulty; and temperance, which moderates the pull of pleasures (CCC 1805–1809). The word “cardinal” comes from the Latin for hinge: other virtues hang on them.', 'The three theological virtues, faith, hope and charity, come from God and are directed to God (CCC 1812–1829). We cannot give them to ourselves; they are infused at Baptism and grow through prayer, sacraments and acts. The greatest is charity (1 Corinthians 13:13). The fruits of the Holy Spirit in Galatians 5:22–23, such as joy, peace and patience, are the visible signs.', 'How to grow: acts become habits, so small repeated acts matter; vices are the opposite habits and also grow through repetition; grace perfects nature and does not replace effort (CCC 1810–1811); and we need friends, good models and the sacraments. The Church’s oldest advice is to start small, to aim at one virtue at a time, to expect to fail, and to return to Confession.'],
   talk=['Which of the four cardinal virtues is your strongest and which is your weakest?', 'What habit have you built that is now easy but once was hard?', 'Which saint models the virtue you most want?'],
   misc=['“Virtue is just following rules.”', '“Either you are born patient or you are not.”'],
   quiz=['Name the four cardinal virtues.|Faith, hope, charity, humility|Prudence, justice, fortitude, temperance|Love, joy, peace, patience|Chastity, poverty, obedience, silence|1|CCC 1805.', 'What is the greatest of the theological virtues?|Faith|Hope|Charity|Prudence|2|1 Corinthians 13:13.', 'How are virtues built?|By repeated good acts, with God’s grace|By wishing|Only by birth|By one decision|0|CCC 1810.'],
   life='Choose one virtue and one small act each day for a week. Each night, mark whether you did it, without judgment.', saint='Pier Giorgio Frassati: a joyful young Italian mountaineer who served the poor of Turin in secret and died of polio at twenty-four.',
   deeper=['room:desales-devout-life'], mem='“But the fruit of the Spirit is love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, gentleness, self-control.” (Galatians 5:22–23)', hook='Ask: “What is a habit you have that you did not have five years ago? How did it start?”', assess='Ask each person to name the cardinal and theological virtues and give one concrete action to grow in one.',
   tryit=['Name the virtue you most need and why.', 'Write three small acts that would build it.', 'Check in with a friend at the end of the week.'],
   saintStory='Pier Giorgio Frassati was born on April 6, 1901, in Turin, Italy, to a wealthy family: his father, Alfredo, founded the newspaper La Stampa and became an ambassador. Pier Giorgio was not a gifted student and struggled at school, but was known for his laughter and love of practical jokes. He loved mountaineering, skiing and horseback riding, and led friends in the “Shady Characters,” a group that hiked the Alps, prayed the rosary on the trail and carved the group’s motto into the rocks.\nHe joined the Saint Vincent de Paul Society and spent his free time visiting the poor in their dingy rooms in Turin, bringing food and medicine, and paying rent from his allowance. He gave his train ticket money to the poor and rode third class, then ran home. He was active in Catholic Action and politics, opposed Mussolini’s fascists, and once protected a banner with a broken pole when police attacked a demonstration. He received Communion daily and prayed before the Blessed Sacrament at night.\nIn 1925 he contracted polio, most likely from the sick he visited. While he was dying, his main concern was to remember the loan of a poor family; the day before he died, he scribbled a note to a friend asking him to take care of it. He died on July 4, 1925, aged twenty-four. Over 4,000 of the poor of Turin came to his funeral, a surprise to his family. He was beatified in 1990 by John Paul II, who called him a man of the eight Beatitudes.',
   script=dict(open='Draw two columns on the board labelled “Habits I built” and “Habits I want.” Invite each person to name one for each. Say, “Tonight we look at how the Church speaks about habits that make us good.”',
     teach=['Say: “The Catechism begins its treatment of virtue with a simple definition: a virtue is a habitual and firm disposition to do the good (CCC 1803). Note the two words: habitual, meaning it becomes second nature, and firm, meaning stable. The virtues let us not just do good acts, but give the best of ourselves. The opposite is vice. St Gregory of Nyssa said that the goal of a virtuous life is to become like God (CCC 1803).”', 'Say: “The four cardinal virtues (CCC 1805–1809): prudence, the charioteer of the virtues, which applies principles to the case; justice, which gives God and neighbor their due, including the right of workers; fortitude, which gives courage to resist temptation and endure hardship, up to martyrdom; temperance, which moderates our attraction to pleasures. The three theological virtues (CCC 1812–1829): faith, hope and charity. They are gifts: only God can give them. We receive them at Baptism and exercise them by acts. The gifts and fruits of the Spirit (CCC 1830–1832) crown them.”', 'Say: “How to grow. First, choose one virtue. Second, plan small, specific, repeatable acts; the Little Way of Thérèse is a model. Third, expect to fail and begin again; return to Confession. Fourth, find a companion; Pier Giorgio had his Shady Characters. Fifth, imitate a model: read a saint’s life. Sixth, pray for the virtue. Grace does not replace effort; it makes effort fruitful (CCC 1810). Aristotle taught that virtue is a mean between two extremes; Christians add that it is a gift which makes us like Christ. Have each person write their plan and share it with a partner.”'],
     ask=['What is a virtue? (A habitual and firm disposition to do the good.)', 'What are the cardinal and theological virtues? (Prudence, justice, fortitude, temperance; faith, hope, charity.)', 'How do we grow in virtue? (Small repeated acts, grace, companions, sacraments.)'],
     practice=['Each person names one virtue to grow and writes three small acts.', 'In pairs, share your plan and agree on a check-in.', 'Read a short passage about Pier Giorgio or another saint and name the virtue it shows.'],
     close='Pray the Act of Faith, Hope and Love together. Invite each person to a brief check-in next week.')),
 L('The Commandments and Beatitudes as a way of life', 'The Ten Commandments are God’s gift to a people he had freed, and Jesus gathers them into the love of God and neighbor. The Beatitudes go further: they describe the happiness he calls us to, and the shape of a life in him.', '1716,1717,1718,1719,1720,1721,1722,1723,1724,1725,1726,1727,1728,1729,2052,2053,2054,2055,2056,2057,2058,2059,2060,2061,2062,2063,2064,2065,2066,2067,2068,2069,2070,2071,2072,2073,2074', 'Matthew 5:1-12', 'commandments beatitudes covenant moral-law sin', 'Are the commandments a list of rules, or a path to freedom and happiness?', 'Read Exodus 20:1–17 and Matthew 5:1–12. Write which commandment each Beatitude seems to deepen.', '',
   teach=['The Decalogue was given after the Exodus, not before it (Exodus 20:2). God saves first and then gives the law as the shape of life in freedom (CCC 2057–2063). The first three commandments concern love of God, the other seven love of neighbor; Jesus sums them as love of God with all one’s heart and love of neighbor as oneself (Matthew 22:37–40; CCC 2055).', 'The Beatitudes (CCC 1716–1729) are the heart of Jesus’ preaching. They respond to the natural desire for happiness that God has put in us, and describe the way of life, and its reward, for his disciples: poverty of spirit, mourning, meekness, hunger for righteousness, mercy, purity of heart, peacemaking and endurance of persecution. They are a self-portrait of Christ and a call to follow him.', 'Together they give a path. The commandments mark the minimum without which love cannot live, such as not killing, stealing or lying; the Beatitudes show the full height of love. Jesus said he came not to abolish the law but to fulfil it (Matthew 5:17). The Church’s practice is to use the Commandments in the examination of conscience and the Beatitudes as a picture of holiness. Obedience is not servile; it is the response of someone who has been loved.'],
   talk=['Which commandment do you find most countercultural today?', 'Which Beatitude surprises you most?', 'Why might the Church say the commandments are a path to freedom?'],
   misc=['“The commandments are arbitrary restrictions.”', '“The Beatitudes are only for saints.”'],
   quiz=['When was the Decalogue given in relation to the Exodus?|Before it|After God had freed Israel|At the Fall|During the Exile|1|Exodus 20:2.', 'How did Jesus sum up the commandments?|Be good|Love of God and love of neighbor|Obey the Church|Avoid sin|1|Matthew 22:37–40.', 'What do the Beatitudes describe?|The path of happiness and the shape of discipleship|Sunday rules|Taxes|Medieval customs|0|CCC 1716–1717.'],
   life='Pray one Beatitude each day this week, asking how it applies to you, and do one concrete thing to live it.', saint='Frédéric Ozanam: a young Sorbonne professor who answered a challenge to show the faith’s fruits by founding a conference of charity, which became the Saint Vincent de Paul Society.',
   deeper=[], mem='“Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God.” (Matthew 5:8)', hook='Ask: “What do you think most people mean by ‘happiness’? What does Jesus mean in Matthew 5?”', assess='Ask each person to summarize the structure of the Ten Commandments and say how the Beatitudes go beyond them.',
   tryit=['Read Exodus 20 and Matthew 5 side by side.', 'Match each Beatitude to a commandment it deepens.', 'Choose one Beatitude to practice this week.'],
   saintStory='Antoine-Frédéric Ozanam was born on April 23, 1813, in Milan, the fifth of fourteen children of a French physician, and grew up in Lyon. As a teenager he went through a crisis of faith, helped by his philosophy teacher, Abbé Noirot. At eighteen he went to Paris to study law at the Sorbonne, and later took a doctorate in literature. He was a gifted student and was soon a leader among the Catholic students.\nIn 1833, at twenty, he was challenged in a debate by students who said that Christianity was dead and had done nothing for the poor. “Show us your works,” they said. Ozanam and a handful of friends gathered under the guidance of Emmanuel Bailly, a printer, and began visiting the poor in their homes, bringing wood, bread and friendship. They took the name of Vincent de Paul. The Society grew rapidly and now operates in more than 130 countries.\nHe became a professor of foreign literature at the Sorbonne, married Amélie Soulacroix in 1841, and had a daughter, Marie. He wrote on Dante and the Franciscans and worked to show that the Church was on the side of the poor and of democracy. During the 1848 revolution he helped found a newspaper, L’Ère Nouvelle. He died on September 8, 1853, aged forty, after a long illness. He was beatified in 1997 by John Paul II, and his feast is September 9.',
   script=dict(open='Ask a direct question: “If someone asked you to name one thing the Church says people must not do, what would you say? Now one thing she says they should do?” Write both lists and look at the gap. Say, “Tonight we see how the Church holds both.”',
     teach=['Say: “Start with the story. The Ten Commandments (CCC 2052–2557) are given in Exodus 20 after the Lord has brought Israel out of Egypt: ‘I am the Lord your God, who brought you out of the land of Egypt.’ The Law is a gift to a freed people, a way of keeping freedom. The Catechism organizes them: the first three are about love of God (no other gods, his name, the Sabbath); the other seven about love of neighbor (parents, life, chastity, property, truth, and the two commandments on coveting). Jesus teaches that they are summed up in love (Matthew 22:37–40; CCC 2055). The rich young man in Matthew 19 is told, ‘If you would enter life, keep the commandments,’ and then invited to more.”', 'Say: “The Beatitudes (CCC 1716–1729) answer the question every human being asks: what will make me happy? Jesus answers with eight paradoxes: poor in spirit, mourning, meek, hungry for righteousness, merciful, pure in heart, peacemakers, persecuted. The Church has called them a portrait of Christ and a description of charity (CCC 1717). The happiness is promised as the vision of God and the Kingdom (CCC 1720–1724). The commandments say what love requires at a minimum; the Beatitudes show what it can become.”', 'Say: “How to use them. First, as an examination of conscience: go through the Ten Commandments one by one, interpreting them with the Catechism’s detail. Second, as a mirror: read the Beatitudes each week and ask which one you most resist. Third, as a path: choose one concrete act of mercy, one of peace, one of purity. Fourth, remember grace: the Sermon on the Mount cannot be lived by willpower alone (CCC 1996–2005). Fifth, notice the companion: the works of mercy are the Beatitudes in action. Frédéric Ozanam answered ‘show us your works’ by visiting the poor.”'],
     ask=['What is the order of the Ten Commandments? (Three about God, seven about neighbor.)', 'How did Jesus sum them up? (Love of God and neighbor.)', 'What do the Beatitudes show? (The happiness God offers and the shape of life in Christ.)'],
     practice=['Read Exodus 20:1–17 aloud in turns. Note the opening line.', 'In pairs, match each Beatitude with the commandment it seems to deepen.', 'Plan one concrete act of mercy, to start this week, and name a partner to check on it.'],
     close='Pray the Beatitudes together, slowly, as a litany: after each, answer “Lord, make me like you.” Close with the Our Father.')),
 ]),
]
